00:34:47.540 --> 00:35:05.600
<v Ira Glass>Act Four, Pre K-O. To understand this next story, which is definitely a story about getting away with it in a big, big way, I first need to give you some background about preschool. In recent years, there've been more and more studies showing that preschool is one of the best investments a country can make in children's education.
<v Ira Glass>第四幕，“学前班”。要理解接下来的这个故事，这绝对是一个关于如何大规模地侥幸成功的故事，我首先需要给你们一些关于学前班的背景。近年来，越来越多的研究表明，学前班是国家在儿童教育方面能做的最好的投资之一。

00:35:05.600 --> 00:35:26.090
<v Ira Glass>Probably the most famous piece of research on this is an old one called the Perry Preschool Project. This study began in the 1960s. They took a bunch of poor kids from Ypsilanti, Michigan, put half of them into a preschool program and then the other half just led their normal lives. And then after a year of that, both of those groups went back into the same public school system.
<v Ira Glass>关于这个研究最著名的可能是一个叫“佩里学前教育项目”的旧研究。这项研究始于20世纪60年代。他们从密歇根州伊普西兰蒂找了一群贫困孩子，把其中一半送进学前教育项目，另一半则照常生活。一年之后，这两组孩子都回到了同一个公立学校系统。

00:35:26.090 --> 00:35:52.580
<v Ira Glass>And then every so often, researchers would follow up with these kids to see how they were doing from the two groups. They're following them still even now, and the results have been amazing. At age 27, the kids who got preschool were half as likely to have gotten arrested. They earned an average of 50% more per month. They were 50% more likely to have a savings account, 20% more likely to have a car. Preschool kids got sick less often, were unemployed less often, sentenced to incarceration less often.
<v Ira Glass>之后每隔一段时间，研究人员就会追踪这些孩子，看看他们的情况如何。即使到现在，他们仍在追踪这些孩子，结果非常惊人。在27岁时，接受过学前教育的孩子被捕的可能性只有另一组的一半。他们平均每月收入高出50%。他们拥有储蓄账户的可能性高出50%，拥有汽车的可能性高出20%。学前班的孩子生病频率较低，失业频率较低，被判入狱的频率也较低。

00:35:52.580 --> 00:36:00.410
<v Ira Glass>Other studies show similar results, not just for poor kids, but for other kids, too. They do better if they have preschool.
<v Ira Glass>其他研究也显示了类似的结果，不仅针对贫困儿童，也针对其他儿童。如果他们接受过学前教育，他们表现得更好。

00:36:00.410 --> 00:36:21.990
<v Ira Glass>And the very first state to choose to make free, high quality preschool available to almost every four-year-old in the state and then actually delivered on that promise turns out to be a surprising one. In fact, only a handful of states have joined it. Alex Blumberg reports that the story of how the state came to lead the nation in preschool access is all about getting away with it.
<v Ira Glass>事实证明，第一个选择向州内几乎每个四岁儿童提供免费、高质量学前教育，并且真正兑现了这一承诺的州，是一个令人惊讶的州。事实上，只有少数几个州加入了。亚历克斯·布鲁姆伯格报道说，该州如何领导全国学前教育普及的故事完全是关于“侥幸成功”的。

00:36:21.990 --> 00:36:31.140
<v Ira Glass>Quick reminder before we start-- today's show is a rerun from a few years ago. Since we did this story, some details of Oklahoma's political landscape have changed. Here's Alex.
<v Ira Glass>在开始之前快速提醒一下——今天的节目是几年前的重播。自从我们做了这个故事以来，俄克拉荷马州的政治格局发生了一些变化。这是亚历克斯的报道。

00:36:31.140 --> 00:36:43.200
<v Alex Blumberg>The state is Oklahoma. And to show you just how surprising that is, consider what happened to a guy named Ron Peters. Peters was a freshman legislator in the Oklahoma State House of Representatives, a Republican.
<v Alex Blumberg>这个州是俄克拉荷马州。为了让你了解这有多令人惊讶，看看一个叫罗恩·彼得斯的人发生了什么。彼得斯是俄克拉荷马州众议院的一名共和党新任议员。

00:36:43.200 --> 00:37:01.080
<v Alex Blumberg>One of his party leaders, a veteran legislator, came up to him one day and said, the governor, also a Republican, appointed this task force to look into the issue of early childhood education. They came up with some recommendations. The recommendations were put into this bill. The governor wants us to pass it. Will you be in charge of making that happen?
<v Alex Blumberg>他的一位政党领袖，一位资深议员，有一天走过来对他说，州长（也是共和党人）任命了一个特别工作组来研究幼儿教育问题。他们提出了一些建议。这些建议被写进了这个法案。州长想让我们通过它。你愿意负责让它实现吗？

00:37:01.080 --> 00:37:08.280
<v Ron Peters>I mean, it was a pretty mundane bill to start out with. It set up a public-private partnership, set up a private foundation--
<v Ron Peters>我的意思是，起初这是一个相当平凡的法案。它建立了一个公共与私营部门的合作伙伴关系，建立了一个私人基金会——

00:37:08.280 --> 00:37:25.050
<v Alex Blumberg>Ron Peters said sure. The bill seemed easy to pass and pretty non-controversial. There were no state funds involved. It was all private money. The idea was simply to set up a nonprofit to publicize the value of early childhood education, so more parents would consider it. So he introduced the bill on the House floor.
<v Alex Blumberg>罗恩·彼得斯答应了。该法案看起来很容易通过，而且相当没有争议。不涉及任何州资金。全是私人资金。这个想法仅仅是建立一个非营利组织来宣传幼儿教育的价值，以便更多的父母考虑它。所以他在众议院提出了该法案。

00:37:25.050 --> 00:37:41.900
<v Ron Peters>I didn't really think anybody would debate it. I mean, how do you debate-- just the private sector was going to raise money to fund the awareness program, so I mean, you couldn't argue about spending state money. But when they said will there be debate, about seven hands on my side of the aisle went up to debate against it.
<v Ron Peters>我真的没认为有人会辩论它。我是说，你如何辩论——只是私营部门要筹集资金来资助意识项目，所以我的意思是，你不能争论花州政府的钱。但当他们说是否会有辩论时，我这边大约有七个人举手反对。

00:37:41.900 --> 00:37:55.220
<v Ron Peters>This is a nanny state bill. The government's going to come take our kids and raise them for us. We think kids should be raised by their mom and dad.
<v Ron Peters>这是一个“保姆国家”法案。政府要来把我们的孩子带走，帮我们抚养他们。我们认为孩子应该由他们的爸爸妈妈抚养。

00:37:55.220 --> 00:38:04.830
<v Ron Peters>When I was elected, I thought I was Rush Limbaugh incarnate. And then I got to the legislature, it was like, who the heck are those people way over there? So--
<v Ron Peters>当我当选时，我以为我就是拉什·林博的化身。然后我到了立法机构，就像是，哇，那边那些人到底是谁啊？所以——

00:38:04.830 --> 00:38:06.540
<v Alex Blumberg>You didn't think there was anybody right of you, huh?
<v Alex Blumberg>你没认为有人比你更右了吗，嗯？

00:38:06.540 --> 00:38:15.710
<v Ron Peters>No. Oh, no. No, but there ended up the whole bunch of them.
<v Ron Peters>不。哦，不。不，但最后确实有一大群人。

00:38:15.710 --> 00:38:44.960
<v Ron Peters>I got it passed. And it passed the House, passed the Senate. And then all the people started getting letters from the far-right wing, saying to the governor, this is a nanny state bill. Why don't we just take our kids from the hospital and take them to you to raise for us? And he got scared a little bit, I guess, and he ended up vetoeing his own task force bill.
<v Ron Peters>我让它通过了。它在众议院通过，在参议院通过。然后所有人都开始收到来自极右翼的信件，告诉州长，这是一个“保姆国家”法案。为什么我们不干脆从医院把孩子带走，直接交给你们来帮我们抚养？我想他有点害怕了，最后否决了他自己的特别工作组法案。

00:38:44.960 --> 00:38:46.920
<v Alex Blumberg>Wait a minute.
<v Alex Blumberg>等一下。

00:38:46.920 --> 00:38:52.620
<v Ron Peters>Oh, I was terribly upset.
<v Ron Peters>哦，我非常难过。

00:38:52.620 --> 00:39:16.170
<v Alex Blumberg>Actually, the governor only threatened a veto, but it was enough to kill Peter's bill that time around. And that is how unlikely it is to pass universal public preschool in Oklahoma. A bill that simply suggests that the state might think it's a good idea for kids to go to preschool-- a bill, remember, that doesn't involve any state money and doesn't actually set up any new government programs-- that bill gets attacked as socialism and scuttled.
<v Alex Blumberg>实际上，州长只威胁要否决，但这足以让彼得斯的法案在那次流产了。这就是在俄克拉荷马州通过全民公立学前班的可能性有多小。一个仅仅建议州政府认为让孩子上学前班是个好主意——记住，一个不涉及任何州资金、也没有真正建立任何新政府项目的法案——却被攻击为社会主义，并被取消了。

00:39:16.170 --> 00:39:45.190
<v Alex Blumberg>Oklahoma is arguably the most conservative state in the country. It's got a Republican House, a Republican Senate, a Republican governor. Four out of its five representatives to Congress are Republicans, as are both its senators. One of those senators, Tom Coburn, was named the most conservative senator in Congress by National Review magazine. And if you need more proof, consider this-- Oklahoma was the only state in the entire country in 2008 where not one single county voted for Barack Obama.
<v Alex Blumberg>俄克拉荷马州可以说是全国最保守的州。它有共和党控制的众议院、参议院和州长。它的五名国会代表中有四名是共和党人，两名参议员也是。其中一名参议员汤姆·科伯恩被《国家评论》杂志评为国会中最保守的参议员。如果你需要更多证据，考虑一下——俄克拉荷马州是2008年全国唯一一个没有任何一个县投票支持巴拉克·奥巴马的州。

00:39:45.190 --> 00:40:06.600
<v Alex Blumberg>So how in the world did anyone get the conservative state of Oklahoma to agree to a dramatic expansion of the state's public education system in preschool, something that many conservatives would oppose? How did anyone get away with that? The answer-- universal public preschool came to Oklahoma not as part of a bold evidence based campaign, it was snuck in, in the dark.
<v Alex Blumberg>那么，到底是怎么有人让保守的俄克拉荷马州同意大幅扩展该州的公立幼儿教育系统，也就是许多保守派会反对的事情？有人是怎么侥幸做到的？答案——全民公立学前班进入俄克拉荷马州并不是作为一个大胆的循证运动的一部分，而是被在暗地里偷偷引入的。

00:40:06.600 --> 00:40:43.860
<v Alex Blumberg>And to give you a sense of just how secretly it happened, consider this rather amazing fact. At the time Ron Peters was getting his little bill squashed by his own party in the legislature, universal preschool was already in place in Oklahoma. It had been voted into law three years earlier, voted into law by many of the same people who were accusing Ron Peters of socialism. And what's more, they had probably voted for that law without even realizing it, which is exactly the way that people who brought public preschool to Oklahoma wanted it.
<v Alex Blumberg>为了让你了解它发生得有多隐秘，考虑这个相当惊人的事实。在罗恩·彼得斯在立法机构被他自己的政党否决他的小法案时，全民学前班已经在这个州实施了。它是在三年前投票通过成为法律的，投票赞成的很多人正是那些指责罗恩·彼得斯是社会主义者的人。而且更重要的是，他们可能在根本没意识到这一点的情况下就为该法案投了票，这正是把公立学前班带到俄克拉荷马州的人所想要的。

00:40:43.860 --> 00:41:00.990
<v Alex Blumberg>The story begins in the late 1990s, and it begins with a seemingly unrelated issue. School superintendents around Oklahoma had discovered a loophole in the state's school funding formula and were using that loophole to run a little scheme that involved kindergarten classes and huge amounts of money.
<v Alex Blumberg>故事始于20世纪90年代末，始于一个看似无关的问题。俄克拉荷马州各地的学校主管们发现了一个州学校拨款公式中的漏洞，并利用这个漏洞运行了一个小计划，涉及幼儿园班级和巨额资金。

00:41:00.990 --> 00:41:27.600
<v Alex Blumberg>So the scheme was it was legal in Oklahoma for schools to have either a full day or a half-day kindergarten program. The loophole was that the schools got paid the same amount from the state either way. And so what school districts began doing was running two half-day programs and collecting twice as much money. And if they couldn't find enough five-year-olds to fill two kindergarten classes, they would pad their numbers with four-year-olds.
<v Alex Blumberg>所以这个计划是，在俄克拉荷马州，学校拥有全日制或半日制幼儿园项目是合法的。漏洞是，无论哪种方式，学校从州政府获得的拨款都是一样的。所以学区开始做的是经营两个半日制项目，并获得两倍的钱。如果他们找不到足够的五岁孩子来填满两个幼儿园班级，他们就会用四岁的孩子来凑数。

00:41:27.600 --> 00:41:31.500
<v Lloyd Snow>We got right in the middle of that very, very early.
<v Lloyd Snow>我们非常、非常早就处于这件事的中心。

00:41:31.500 --> 00:41:40.140
<v Alex Blumberg>Lloyd Snow was a school superintendent at this time who took advantage of this loophole-- ran two half-day kindergartens, enrolled a bunch of four-year-olds, and got paid double from the state.
<v Alex Blumberg>劳埃德·斯诺是当时的一位学校主管，他利用了这个漏洞——经营了两个半日制幼儿园，招收了一群四岁的孩子，并从州政府那里获得了双倍的拨款。

00:41:40.140 --> 00:42:07.830
<v Lloyd Snow>It was like a no-brainer to not only be able to do this, put it in play, and then have money left over for other so desperately needed underfunded educational processes. I mean, it was a good thing, and we haven't had very many good things in public schools in my estimation. So I didn't blink twice.
<v Lloyd Snow>这简直不需要动脑子，不仅能这样做，把它付诸实施，而且还有多余的钱用于其他急需的、资金不足的教育流程。我的意思是，这是一件好事，据我估计，公立学校里没发生过多少好事。所以我没眨眼就做了。

00:42:07.830 --> 00:42:21.960
<v Alex Blumberg>All around the state, the scheme spread. Four-year-olds were like gold, and the extra money they brought in went to everything from football programs to new school equipment, even new buildings. That's what happened in the district of Joe Eddins, a state legislator around this time.
<v Alex Blumberg>在全州范围内，这个计划传播开了。四岁的孩子就像金子一样，他们带来的额外资金被用于一切事物，从足球项目到新学校设备，甚至新建筑。这就是乔·埃丁斯所在的学区发生的事情，他当时是一位州议员。

00:42:21.960 --> 00:42:51.360
<v Joe Eddins>There was so much money came with these children, the school district bought what we call portable classrooms-- a big mobile home that you clunk it together and you've got a nice classroom prefab here. They bought the buildings, paid cash for them with the money the first year. Yeah, I mean, it's that much gravy, and then from then on, they got to use the buildings, in essence, free.
<v Joe Eddins>这些孩子带来了这么多钱，学区购买了我们所谓的便携式教室——一个巨大的移动房屋，你把它们拼在一起，就是一个不错的预制教室。他们买了这些建筑，第一年就用这笔钱付清了。是的，我的意思是，利润就是那么多，从那以后，他们就能使用这些建筑，本质上是免费的。

00:42:51.360 --> 00:43:16.840
<v Alex Blumberg>That might sound like Joe was happy about this, but he wasn't. This seemed like a loophole that people were abusing. And so for two years, he tried to close it, to keep four-year-olds out of kindergarten. But he got nowhere. And Joe Eddins, he becomes central to the story of universal preschool in Oklahoma. You'll hear a lot more from him later after he meets this next guy, another main character in the saga, a guy named Bob Harbison.
<v Alex Blumberg>这听起来乔对此很高兴，但他并不高兴。这看起来像是一个人们在滥用的漏洞。所以两年来，他试图关闭它，把四岁的孩子挡在幼儿园门外。但他没成功。乔·埃丁斯，他成为了俄克拉荷马州全民学前班故事的核心。稍后你会在他遇到接下来这个人之后听到他更多的故事，这是故事中的另一个主要角色，一个叫鲍勃·哈比森的人。

00:43:16.840 --> 00:43:33.910
<v Alex Blumberg>Bob Harbison is a self-described corporate guy-- conservative suits, tortoiseshell glasses. He was for many years a senior manager at TWA, and after that, an executive in the oil and gas industry. He was also friends with people on the board of the Tulsa Chamber of Commerce. And one day--
<v Alex Blumberg>鲍勃·哈比森是一个自称的“企业人”——保守的西装，玳瑁眼镜。他多年来一直是TWA航空公司的资深经理，此后是石油和天然气行业的高管。他也是塔尔萨商会董事会成员的朋友。有一天——

00:43:33.910 --> 00:44:03.280
<v Bob Harbison>A banker friend of mine that was the incoming president of the Chamber brought me up. He said, I want to talk to you about a project we've been working on at the Chamber. And he said that it started in 1990. They had put together a little task force to evaluate workforce development issues because Tulsa was going through a pretty explosive growth period.
<v Bob Harbison>我的一位银行家朋友，即商会的候任主席，提到了我。他说，我想和你谈谈我们在商会一直致力于的一个项目。他说这始于1990年。他们成立了一个小特别工作组来评估劳动力发展问题，因为塔尔萨正经历一个相当爆炸性的增长期。

00:44:03.280 --> 00:44:18.460
<v Alex Blumberg>The banker friend explained that local businesses in Tulsa had a problem. They were finding it harder and harder to hire employees with enough skills and abilities to do the job. The Chamber had been looking into the reasons for this. Was it colleges and universities not training people properly? High schools?
<v Alex Blumberg>银行家朋友解释说，塔尔萨当地的企业遇到了一个问题。他们越来越难雇用到具备足够技能和能力来完成工作的员工。商会一直在调查其中的原因。是大学没有好好培训人吗？高中呢？

00:44:18.460 --> 00:44:29.560
<v Bob Harbison>They kept working back to this notion of increasing numbers of children not being ready for school.
<v Bob Harbison>他们一直追溯到这个概念，即越来越多的孩子没有为上学做好准备。

00:44:29.560 --> 00:44:40.120
<v Alex Blumberg>This banker friend handed Bob a cardboard box filled with research and reports on early childhood education and said, here, look this over and see what you think we should do.
<v Alex Blumberg>这位银行家朋友递给鲍勃一个装满幼儿教育研究和报告的纸板箱，说，来，看看这个，看看你认为我们应该做什么。

00:44:40.120 --> 00:45:00.160
<v Bob Harbison>Working through this cardboard box of materials in a cubicle at the Chamber, they and the staff had accumulated literature on this subject. And I became familiar with the Perry Preschool study.
<v Bob Harbison>在商会的一个隔间里翻阅这个装满材料的纸板箱，他们和工作人员积累了关于这个主题的文献。我熟悉了“佩里学前教育项目”的研究。

00:45:00.160 --> 00:45:09.650
<v Alex Blumberg>And do you remember reading those studies, like, was that sort of an aha moment where you're reading these long-term effects of preschool? Do you remember your mindset then as you were reading through?
<v Alex Blumberg>你还记得读那些研究吗，那算是那种“顿悟时刻”，当你读到学前教育的长期影响时？你还记得读这些时你的心态吗？

00:45:09.650 --> 00:45:20.110
<v Bob Harbison>Well, I tended to frame things in terms of my business experience. It was just sort of automatic.
<v Bob Harbison>嗯，我倾向于用我的商业经验来框架事物。这算是自动的。

00:45:20.110 --> 00:45:42.620
<v Alex Blumberg>Harbison said he thought back to his career in the airline industry, where one of the worst things you can do is fly a plane with an empty seat. But a kid who comes to kindergarten too far behind, statistically, that kid might never catch up. He's occupying that seat in first, second, third grade, in middle school and high school. But the seat might as well be empty for all the good it's doing the kid sitting in it.
<v Alex Blumberg>哈比森说，他回想起了他在航空业的职业生涯，在那里你能做的最糟糕的事情之一就是开一架空座的飞机。但一个上幼儿园时远远落后的孩子，从统计学上讲，那个孩子可能永远追不上。他在小学、初中和高中的一、二、三年级占据着那个座位。但对于那个坐在上面、一点益处也没得到的孩子来说，那个座位简直就是空着的。

00:45:42.620 --> 00:45:55.510
<v Alex Blumberg>And also, he thought when you look at the statistics of what happens to a lot of at-risk kids when they get out of school, they end up costing the government a lot of money. They end up incarcerated or on public assistance-- bad outcomes for them and for society.
<v Alex Blumberg>而且，他还认为，当你观察很多高风险孩子在离开学校后会发生什么统计数据时，他们最终会花费政府很多钱。他们最终会被监禁或依靠公共救济——对他们自己和社会来说都是坏的结果。

00:45:55.510 --> 00:46:10.190
<v Bob Harbison>I always thought about if there was a business or a group of businesses and they had agreed to pay for all the outcomes, they would be spending money on prevention.
<v Bob Harbison>我总是想，如果有企业或企业集团，他们同意支付所有的后果，他们就会把钱花在预防上。

00:46:10.190 --> 00:46:23.910
<v Alex Blumberg>This cardboard box made a big impression on Bob Harbison, who, in his quiet way, became something of a crusader for early childhood education, making the case for it to anyone who would listen, including, sometime in the late 1990s, Joe Eddins.
<v Alex Blumberg>这个纸板箱给鲍勃·哈比森留下了深刻印象，他以安静的方式，成为了幼儿教育的十字军，向任何愿意听的人推销这个案子，包括在20世纪90年代末的某个时候，对乔·埃丁斯。

00:46:23.910 --> 00:46:36.950
<v Alex Blumberg>Now, remember, Joe was this legislator. He had this bill trying to close that loophole and keep four-year-olds out of kindergarten. Bob Harbison started making the case that we don't want fewer four-year-olds in public school-- we want more of them. Here's Joe.
<v Alex Blumberg>现在，记住，乔是这位议员。他有个法案试图关闭那个漏洞，把四岁的孩子挡在幼儿园门外。鲍勃·哈比森开始推销一个案例，说我们不是想要更少的四岁孩子上公立学校——我们想要更多。听听乔怎么说。

00:46:36.950 --> 00:47:11.930
<v Joe Eddins>Bob Harbison is-- well, you know how smooth Bob Harbison is, isn't he? You know what I mean? I never knew I was being lobbied, if I was being lobbied. I just thought, well, this Bob Harbison's telling me the things I need to know, which, incidentally, Bob Harbison was telling the truth. But that was the key that Bob Harbison found me a willing pupil and taught me all I know about four-year-olds.
<v Joe Eddins>鲍勃·哈比森是——嗯，你知道鲍勃·哈比森有多圆滑，不是吗？你知道我的意思吗？我从来不知道我正在被游说，如果我确实被游说了的话。我只是想，嗯，这个鲍勃·哈比森在告诉我我需要知道的事情，顺便说一下，鲍勃·哈比森说的是真的。但这成了关键，鲍勃·哈比森发现我是一个愿意学习的学生，并教了我所有关于四岁孩子的事情。

00:47:11.930 --> 00:47:27.140
<v Alex Blumberg>Over the course of several meetings, Bob shared with Joe the contents of that cardboard box-- all those studies and research projects demonstrating the importance of early childhood education. And Joe gradually decided that he needed to change his law and make it much bigger.
<v Alex Blumberg>在几次会议的过程中，鲍勃与乔分享了那个纸板箱的内容——所有那些证明幼儿教育重要性的研究和项目。乔逐渐决定他需要改变他的法律，把它做得更大。

00:47:27.140 --> 00:47:44.980
<v Alex Blumberg>He would still eliminate that four-year-olds in kindergarten for cash scheme, sure. No longer could schools pack their kindergartens with four-year-olds to get extra money from the state. But alongside that, he would provide for an actual program for four-year-olds-- an entirely new grade fully paid for by the state-- public preschool.
<v Alex Blumberg>他当然还会消除那个四岁孩子在幼儿园换现金的计划。学校再也不能把四岁的孩子塞进幼儿园来从州政府获得额外资金。但与此同时，他将为一个四岁孩子的实际项目提供支持——一个完全由州政府全额支付的全新年级——公立学前班。

00:47:44.980 --> 00:48:14.600
<v Alex Blumberg>And it turned out Oklahoma had a very good pilot preschool program with guidelines for choosing the appropriate curriculum and hiring qualified teachers. But this program only existed in a few districts because, according to Eddins, the state didn't provide enough money to pay for it. Eddins estimates the state provided only a third of the money that was actually needed to run one of these programs. Joe Eddins' plan was to take that pilot program and fully fund it, so that any district that wanted it could actually get paid enough to put it in place.
<v Alex Blumberg>事实证明，俄克拉荷马州有一个非常好的试验性学前班项目，有选择适当课程和聘请合格教师的指导方针。但根据埃丁斯的说法，这个项目只存在于少数几个学区，因为州政府没有提供足够的钱来支付它。埃丁斯估计，州政府提供的钱只有真正运行这些项目所需资金的三分之一。乔·埃丁斯的计划是完全资助那个试验项目，这样任何想要它的学区都能真正获得足够的拨款来实施它。

00:48:14.600 --> 00:48:33.900
<v Alex Blumberg>Now there was only one problem with this plan. There was probably no way it could pass-- no way the state legislature would ever agree to pay for a program like this. And so Joe Eddins essentially hid it. He simply offered his amendment again, the one closing the four-year-olds in kindergarten for cash loophole, only with some new language.
<v Alex Blumberg>现在这个计划只有一个问题。可能没法通过——州立法机构绝对不会同意为这样一个项目支付费用。所以乔·埃丁斯本质上把它藏了起来。他只是再次提出了他的修正案，就是关闭那个四岁孩子在幼儿园换现金的漏洞，只是换了一些新的措辞。

00:48:33.900 --> 00:48:52.530
<v Alex Blumberg>A kind of PS in which he proposed a tweak to the state's school funding formula-- made it so that schools got the same amount of funding for preschool that they got for other grades. This was, in fact, a massive overhaul of Oklahoma's public school system, but the actual text of Eddins' amendment looked like nothing special.
<v Alex Blumberg>一种“附言”，他在其中提议对州的学校拨款公式进行微调——使学校获得的学前班拨款与它们在其他年级获得的拨款相同。这实际上是对俄克拉荷马州公立学校系统的巨大改革，但埃丁斯修正案的实际文本看起来并没什么特别之处。

00:48:52.530 --> 00:49:14.820
<v Joe Eddins>The way you amend a bill, the staff takes the sections of law that you're amending and then write your amendments in underlined and line out what you are deleting language. And I just had a few words here and a few words there.
<v Joe Eddins>你修改法案的方式，是工作人员拿出你正在修改的法律章节，然后用下划线写出你的修正案，并划掉你正在删除的语言。我就在这里改了几个词，在那里改了几个词。

00:49:14.820 --> 00:49:32.310
<v Alex Blumberg>Still, there were the traditional and powerful enemies to something like this-- budget hawks and limited government folks, which, as I said, Oklahoma might lead the nation in. Eddins' strategy here-- political sleight of hand. He drew their attention to the stuff in the bill he thought they would like and didn't mention the stuff they wouldn't.
<v Alex Blumberg>尽管如此，还是有针对此类事情的传统且强大的敌人——预算鹰派和有限政府人士，正如我所说的，俄克拉荷马州可能在全国领先。埃丁斯的策略是——政治障眼法。他把他们的注意力引向法案中他认为他们会喜欢的条款，而没提他们不会喜欢的那些。

00:49:32.310 --> 00:49:43.210
<v Joe Eddins>I probably sat down at their desk with each Republican and showed them two or three things in the bill that I thought they ought to know.
<v Joe Eddins>我可能和每个共和党人坐在桌边，向他们展示了法案中两三件我认为他们应该知道的事情。

00:49:43.210 --> 00:49:44.860
<v Alex Blumberg>And what were those things?
<v Alex Blumberg>那些事情是什么？

00:49:44.860 --> 00:50:04.320
<v Joe Eddins>Well, I showed them where we're keeping four-year-olds out of kindergarten, we're saving enormous amounts of money. You can contract with private providers, and they love that. That's all I said.
<v Joe Eddins>好吧，我展示了我们如何把四岁的孩子挡在幼儿园门外，我们节省了巨额资金。你可以与私人提供商签约，他们喜欢这个。我只说了这些。

00:50:04.320 --> 00:50:27.170
<v Alex Blumberg>Now everything he said was true. Eddins' bill did allow state money to go to private daycare centers to teach preschool. Also, his plan was voluntary. School districts didn't have to offer pre-K programs, and parents didn't have to send their kids to one. So it was entirely reasonable to expect that in the beginning, at least, not too many school districts would even take advantage of Eddins' tweak to the funding formula and offer a pre-K program.
<v Alex Blumberg>他说的每件事都是真的。埃丁斯的法案确实允许州资金流向私人日托中心来教授学前教育。此外，他的计划是自愿的。学区不必提供学前班项目，家长也不必把孩子送到那儿。所以完全合理的是，至少在开始时，没有太多的学区会利用埃丁斯的拨款公式调整来提供学前班项目。

00:50:27.170 --> 00:50:45.450
<v Alex Blumberg>And one other thing Eddins had going for him, this four-year-olds in kindergarten for cash scheme, it was growing. And it was feared that the two biggest districts in the state-- Oklahoma City and Tulsa-- were about to get in on it. Eddins says that compared to the sure financial Armageddon that would be, his bill was the cheaper alternative.
<v Alex Blumberg>埃丁斯还有一件有利的事，那个四岁孩子在幼儿园换现金的计划，它在增长。而且人们担心州里最大的两个学区——俄克拉荷马城和塔尔萨——即将参与其中。埃丁斯说，与那将导致的财务末日相比，他的法案是更便宜的替代方案。

00:50:45.450 --> 00:51:14.070
<v Joe Eddins>Everybody could see that my bill was way cheaper than not my bill. If you voted no on my bill and killed it, you are going to have enormous enrollment of four-year-olds in kindergarten. And this was taking an immense amount of money. And all but one voted yes on the bill.
<v Joe Eddins>每个人都能看出我的法案比“不是我的法案”便宜得多。如果你对我的法案投反对票并否决它，你就会有大量四岁的孩子入学幼儿园。这需要巨大的资金。除了一个人之外，所有人都投了赞成票。

00:51:14.070 --> 00:51:35.640
<v Alex Blumberg>Probably because in almost all his discussions with lawmakers about his bill, Eddins left out the main part. You know, the part about how he'd put the state on the hook to pay for an entirely new grade level. He knew almost nobody in any legislature actually reads all the bills, and the number of people who actually understood Oklahoma's school funding formula you could probably count on one hand.
<v Alex Blumberg>这可能是因为在几乎所有他与议员讨论法案的谈话中，埃丁斯都略过了主要部分。你知道，关于他如何让州政府为了一个全新的年级级别买单的那部分。他知道在任何立法机构里几乎没人真正阅读所有的法案，而真正理解俄克拉荷马州学校拨款公式的人你可能一只手就能数过来。

00:51:35.640 --> 00:51:48.390
<v Alex Blumberg>And his strategy worked. It was a huge and costly expansion of the government's role in public education in a state more opposed to costly expansions of government than perhaps any other in the country.
<v Alex Blumberg>他的策略奏效了。这是一个巨大且昂贵的扩张，政府在公立教育中扮演的角色，在一个可能比国内任何其他州都更反对政府昂贵扩张的州里。

00:51:48.390 --> 00:51:58.500
<v Alex Blumberg>Steven Dow is the director of an anti-poverty agency and a longtime early childhood education advocate in Oklahoma. He worked very closely with Joe Eddins to get this law written and passed.
<v Alex Blumberg>史蒂文·多是反贫困机构的主管，也是俄克拉荷马州长期的幼儿教育倡导者。他与乔·埃丁斯密切合作，撰写并推动了这项法律的通过。

00:51:58.500 --> 00:52:12.360
<v Steven Dow>I think it's fair to say that the number of people that understood what was going on in terms of the adjustment to the state aid formula, that most certainly you could count the number of people that were aware of that on one hand.
<v Steven Dow>我认为公平地说，理解州援助公式调整情况的人数，毫无疑问，你能用一只手就数出那些了解这一点的人数。

00:52:12.360 --> 00:52:20.050
<v Alex Blumberg>Now if there had been-- if everybody in the state was aware of it, would it have passed?
<v Alex Blumberg>现在如果存在——如果州里每个人都知道它，它会通过吗？

00:52:20.050 --> 00:52:25.620
<v Steven Dow>I don't know that I would say that I think it's likely that it would have passed.
<v Steven Dow>我不知道我会说我认为它很可能会通过。

00:52:25.620 --> 00:52:29.080
<v Alex Blumberg>Basically, if you guys hadn't snuck this provision in--
<v Alex Blumberg>基本上，如果你们没有把这个条款偷偷放进去——

00:52:29.080 --> 00:52:47.920
<v Steven Dow>Right, I would say had the enactment of universal pre-K not been done through this what I would call stealth campaign, I think it's fair to say that it would not have been embraced.
<v Steven Dow>是的，我会说如果全民学前班的实施不是通过我所说的这种“隐身运动”完成的，我认为公平地说它不会被接受。

00:52:47.920 --> 00:53:07.540
<v Alex Blumberg>The irony is now that the law is in place in Oklahoma, it just adds evidence to the case for preschool. Researchers who studied Tulsa's preschool kids say they enter kindergarten an average of five months ahead in math, seven months ahead in writing, and nine months ahead in reading. And the gains are even more pronounced for at-risk kids who get preschool.
<v Alex Blumberg>具有讽刺意味的是，现在法律在俄克拉荷马州已实施，它只是增加了学前班的证据。研究塔尔萨学前班儿童的研究人员表示，他们进入幼儿园时在数学方面平均领先5个月，写作方面领先7个月，阅读方面领先9个月。对于那些接受学前教育的高风险儿童来说，这种收获更为显著。

00:53:07.540 --> 00:53:15.910
<v Alex Blumberg>Lloyd Snow, the Oklahoma school superintendent, says he didn't need a study to show him that. He could see it the very next year, after his district started offering preschool.
<v Alex Blumberg>俄克拉荷马州学校主管劳埃德·斯诺说，他不需要研究来证明这一点。他在他的学区开始提供学前班后的第二年就能看到。

00:53:15.910 --> 00:53:42.400
<v Ira Glass>I mean, one year after we started, I mean, we had to have department meetings and all kinds of stuff because our kids have better word recognition and letter recognition, better social skills, better hand-eye coordination skills, better-- just almost better everything, as it relates to readiness for school.
<v Ira Glass>我的意思是，我们开始一年后，我的意思是，我们不得不召开部门会议和各种事情，因为我们的孩子有更好的词汇识别和字母识别能力，更好的社交技能，更好的手眼协调能力，更好的——几乎与学校准备状态相关的更好的一切。

00:53:42.400 --> 00:53:52.510
<v Ira Glass>So now, we've got all of our kids from whatever background, I mean, they're voracious learners now. Don't start me with that stuff. So--
<v Ira Glass>所以现在，无论背景如何，我们的孩子都得到了，我的意思是，他们现在是如饥似渴的学习者。别跟我提那些。所以——

00:53:52.510 --> 00:53:53.890
<v Alex Blumberg>I know my ABCs. I know--
<v Alex Blumberg>我知道我的ABC，我知道——

00:53:53.890 --> 00:54:02.050
<v Lloyd Snow>Yeah, yeah, yeah. So that was kind of a game changer for us, and I think most schools.
<v Lloyd Snow>是的，是的，是的。所以这对我们来说是一个游戏规则的改变者，我想对大多数学校也是如此。

00:54:02.050 --> 00:54:19.270
<v Alex Blumberg>A game changer that, according to Joe Eddins, the legislator who arguably did more to change the game than anyone else, would not have been possible without the strange four-year-olds in kindergarten for cash scheme, the scheme that Eddins used as a smokescreen to sneak universal pre-K through a skeptical legislature.
<v Alex Blumberg>一个游戏规则的改变者，根据乔·埃丁斯的说法，这位比任何人都更努力改变规则的立法者，如果没有那种奇怪的“四岁孩子在幼儿园换现金”的计划，这是不可能实现的，埃丁斯把这个计划当作烟幕弹，让全民学前班溜进了一个持怀疑态度的立法机构。

00:54:19.270 --> 00:54:53.020
<v Joe Eddins>I don't see it ever being funded if you had it like other states do, if you had to say, here's a program that we want to implement. Here's how much money it'll take. Woo, where are you going to get the money? OK? If you would have to have a line item of appropriation, nobody would have supported it, except the young mothers. And they have no political clout.
<v Joe Eddins>如果你像其他州那样做，如果你必须说，这里有一个我们想要实施的项目。这是它需要多少钱。呜，你从哪儿弄钱？好吗？如果你必须有一个拨款细目，除了年轻的母亲之外，没人会支持它。而她们没有政治影响力。

00:54:53.020 --> 00:54:57.690
<v Alex Blumberg>What lessons could other states-- say, other states want to try to do something like this--
<v Alex Blumberg>其他州可以学到什么教训——假设其他州想要尝试做这样的事情——

00:54:57.690 --> 00:55:19.710
<v Joe Eddins>They don't have a prayer. They don't have a prayer. Because it's expensive, and state legislatures are run by people that don't want to-- they want to cut programs, not add programs.
<v Joe Eddins>他们没戏。他们没戏。因为这很贵，州立法机构由那些不想——他们想要削减项目，而不是增加项目的人管理。

00:55:19.710 --> 00:55:36.340
<v Alex Blumberg>Not everyone would agree with Eddins on this. In fact, if you talk to experts, the trend around the country is for more and more kids to be enrolled in state-funded preschool programs. States as diverse as Georgia, Iowa, Vermont, and West Virginia are all hard on the heels of Oklahoma, with preschool programs of their own.
<v Alex Blumberg>并非每个人都会同意埃丁斯的观点。事实上，如果你与专家交谈，全国的趋势是越来越多的孩子被纳入州政府资助的学前班项目。像佐治亚州、爱荷华州、佛蒙特州和西弗吉尼亚州这样多元化的州，都在紧随俄克拉荷马州之后，拥有自己的学前班项目。

00:55:36.340 --> 00:56:06.580
<v Alex Blumberg>And as for Oklahoma, parents, says Lloyd Snow, the school superintendent, they love this program. And statistics across the state bear that out. Remember, parents don't have to send their kids to preschool. It's voluntary. But almost 75% of the state's four-year-olds are now enrolled in public preschool. However it arrived, it seems that universal pre-K is here to stay in Oklahoma.
<v Alex Blumberg>至于俄克拉荷马州，学校主管劳埃德·斯诺说，家长们喜欢这个项目。全州的统计数据证实了这一点。记住，家长不必把孩子送到学前班。它是自愿的。但全州近75%的四岁儿童现在都已进入公立学前班。无论它是如何到来的，全民学前班似乎在俄克拉荷马州已扎根。

00:56:06.580 --> 00:56:19.710
<v Ira Glass>Alex Blumberg, he's the founder of Gimlet Media and the host of the podcast, Without Fail. We originally aired this story back when he was on our staff in 2012. Oklahoma's universal preschool program is still going strong.
<v Ira Glass>亚历克斯·布鲁姆伯格，他是Gimlet Media的创始人，也是播客《Without Fail》的主持人。我们在2012年他还在我们员工时首次播出了这个故事。俄克拉荷马州的全民学前班项目仍在强劲发展。

